// The manifest used to be the promise. Now this file is.
//
// `INTERNET` sat in `blockedPermissions` since the app shipped, and that one line was the whole
// enforcement behind "nothing leaves your phone": expo-file-system and expo-image both declare
// the permission, and the manifest is what kept it out of the build. The recap map needs tiles,
// so the line is gone — and a promise enforced by nothing is a claim.
//
// What replaces it is narrower than the manifest and says the same thing. MapLibre fetches its
// tiles natively, from one host named in the privacy policy; **no JavaScript in this app has any
// business opening a socket**, and none ever had — this plugin was written against a codebase
// where the grep found zero calls. The first `fetch` in a future PR is the one that turns one
// host into two without anyone deciding to.
//
// One has since been decided on, deliberately, and it is the single name in the filter below.
// `src/updateCheck.ts` asks api.github.com once a day whether a newer version has been
// published, for the hero who installed the APK by hand and has no store to tell them. It is off
// until switched on, it is named in the privacy policy and under its own Settings row, and it
// downloads nothing.
//
// The header this file used to carry said to delete it rather than exempt anything, on the
// grounds that an exemption list is how a rule stops being read. Deleting it would have left the
// next `fetch` with nothing in its way at all, which is the outcome the rule exists to prevent,
// so the instruction is overruled and replaced by a stricter one: this filter holds **one
// module**, whose name is the host it talks to. A second entry is not a lint change. It is a
// change to what the front page and the policy promise, and it does not land without both.
//
// A second one landed on 25 September 2026, with both of those: `src/cloudSync.ts`, device sync
// to the hero's own Nextcloud or WebDAV server (roadmap 4.18). Its host is the one the hero types
// in, it is off until they sign in there, and it only ever sends files sealed on the device, so
// the server holds bytes it cannot read. The policy's "Syncing your devices" section and the front
// page's privacy band say so.
//
// expo-file-system transfers natively, and those calls are a socket all the same: the front page
// says a lint rule refuses every connection outside these modules, and without the last two
// entries below that sentence was not true.
//
// `language js` like the other three plugins here, which is also why the tile URL lives in a
// `.ts` style module rather than a `.json` one — see docs/designs/map-immersion.md.
language js

`$fn($...)` where {
  $fn <: or {
    `fetch`,
    `XMLHttpRequest`,
    `WebSocket`,
    `EventSource`,
    `navigator.sendBeacon`,
    `File.downloadFileAsync`,
    `$_.upload`,
    `$_.createUploadTask`,
    `File.createDownloadTask`
  },
  $filename <: not r".*__tests__.*",
  $filename <: not r".*src/updateCheck\.ts",
  $filename <: not r".*src/cloudSync\.ts",
  register_diagnostic(span=$fn, message="No network from JavaScript outside src/updateCheck.ts and src/cloudSync.ts. Map tiles are fetched natively by MapLibre; the version check and device sync are the JS requests this app makes; all three are named in the privacy policy and all three are off by default. Anything else breaks the promise the manifest used to enforce. See .biome/plugins/noJsNetwork.grit.")
}
